    "content": "{\n  \"search_id\": \"search_fda4d4db881c8fe966de7082d52024f1\",\n  \"results\": [\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13647254\",\n      \"title\": \"Benchmarking machine learning and molecular dynamics methods for the prediction of the thermophysical properties of organic liquids\",\n      \"publish_date\": \"2026-09-29\",\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Accurate prediction of thermophysical properties is crucial for the rational design of lubricants and cooling fluids. We compile a database of approximately 4700 critically evaluated reference values for the density, viscosity, and thermal conductivity of 835 unique organic molecules in the C10–C50 range.\\nEquilibrium molecular dynamics (EMD) simulations of eight representative molecules reproduce density accurately but show systematic, chemistry-dependent force-field errors for the viscosity and thermal conductivity.\\nToc\\nMachine learning models trained on ∼4700 reference values predict the density, viscosity, and thermal conductivity of lubricant-sized organic molecules, benchmarked against Green–Kubo molecular dynamics simulations of eight representative fluids.\\nIntroduction\\nAll the MD simulations were performed using the LAMMPS software.^102 EMD simulations were employed to calculate the density, viscosity, and thermal conductivity of the eight target molecules at 40 °C and 100 °C, consistent with conditions used to determine lubricant viscosity index values.^8 All simulations were performed using the LOPLS-AA force\\nOf the three properties, density is reproduced most accurately. MD matches the reference values to within 3% for six of the eight molecules at 40 °C (within 4% for seven) and within 6% for seven of the eight at 100 °C (Fig.\\n7a and S10a), consistent with previous benchmarks for linear and branched alkanes.^26 The instantaneous density reaches a stable plateau within approximately 0.2 ns of the 1 ns NPT equilibration (shown for all eight molecules at both temperatures in Fig. S11), and no residual equilibration drift is detectable in the reported values.\\nFour of the eight molecules lie within 25% of the reference values at 40 °C, with squalane under-predicted by 42% and decyl stearate, 4-hexyl-1,7-diphenylheptane, and bis(2-ethylhexyl) sebacate over-predicted by 44, 69, and 137%; at 100 °C four lie within 10%, with decyl stearate (+45%), 7,10,13-trioxanonadecane (−27%), tetraethylene glycol\\nClassical force fields are parameterized primarily for equilibrium thermodynamics, above all liquid density, which they reproduce here to within 4% for seven of eight molecules. On the other hand, these force fields are not fit to transport properties.\\nThe MD benchmark, using a Green–Kubo protocol with quantified trajectory-length convergence, shows that the accuracy of the LOPLS-AA force field is property- and chemistry-dependent. Density is reproduced to within 4% for seven of the eight molecules at 40 °C.\\nThe ML models, trained directly on the target properties, show no comparable bias across the chemical families of this eight-molecule case study: their OOF predictions stay close to the reference values for the density and thermal conductivity of these molecules, with larger but unsystematic errors for viscosity, at an inference cost some seven\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/jres/087/jresv87n4p279_A1b.pdf\",\n      \"title\": \"The Thermal Conductivity of Oxygen - NIST\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"The Thermal Conductivity of Oxygen - NIST\\nnew experimental measurements are presented that cover a large range in density for every isotherm, i.e., 0 to 19 mol/L for 310 K and 0 to 40 mol/L for 78 K.\\nThe new results and a theoretical calculation for the dilute gas [6,7] are used to fashion a new correlation for the thermal conductivity surface of oxygen between 78 and 310 K for\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/jres/086/jresv86n3p293_A1b.pdf\",\n      \"title\": \"Absolute Determination of the  Thermal Conductivity of Argon at Room Temperature and Pressures Up to 68 Mpa\",\n      \"publish_date\": \"2010-02-22\",\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Experimental evidence of a thermal conductivity enhancement near the critical density for a temperature about twice the critical temperature is herein reported.\\nThe difference be- tween the experimental values and the calculated ones sup- ports the existence of a critical point enhancement, as the hard sphere model agrees with the experimental values to within 0.7 mW/m-K or about 3.5 percent at densities below 5 mol/L and about 1.5 percent at densities above 15 mol/L.\\nThe total number of points taken was 112, with an aver· age of four different power levels at each level of density or pressure. Overlap of density range in different working days was done to assess the longer term reproducibility of the instrument.\\n6. Performance of the Instrument\\nThe reproducibility of the instrument is obtained through an intercomparison of experimental points at the same nominal temperature and the same nominal density taken for different heat inputs. Table 2 shows one such set of ex- perimental points obtained on two different days with dif- ferent fillings at a density near 2.9 mol/L (116 kg/m 3 ). The\\nRun Pt Pressure T, Density Thermal Conductivity W/tn-K\\nThe aver- age value for the eight points at Tnom = 300.65 K and enom = 2.9 mol/L is 0.02039 ± 0.00012 W/m-K, the variance of this sample being ± 0.6 percent. The variance was found to be roughly the same for all densities, thus the precision of the instrument is on the order of ± 0.6 percent.\\nHowever, considering the reproducibility to be ± 0.6 percent, and considering that the deviation of a set of AT data from its regression straight line is quite often closer to 0.8 percent, we shall regard the 0.3 percent obtained in the low density extrapo- lation as a fortunate coincidence, and claim an overall accu- racy of ± 1 percent for the\\nvalues of thermal conductivity.\\n7. Results and Analysis of the Data\\nTable 3 presents the 112 points obtained for argon in the density range 0.6 to 20 mol/L (24 to 820 kg/m 3 ). The last col-\\numn in table 3 shows the value of thermal conductivity ad- justed to a nominal temperature of 300.65 K.\\nThe four sets of data agree within their mutual uncertainties of ± 1.0 percent, ± 0.3 percent, ± 2 percent and ± 3 percent re- spectively. Following the well known density dependence of thermal conductivity for moderately dense gases we tried to fit a curve of the type\\n(10)\\nFigure 9 shows the comparison between our smoothed experimental thermal conductivity values and the values predicted by the hard sphere theory, AHs as a function of the ratio Vj V. It can be seen that theory predicts the thermal conductivity of argon to within 0.7 mW/m-K (about 3.5 per- cent at low densities and 1.5 percent at high densities).\\n[12] and Le Neindre, et al. [13] within the mutual uncertainty. The results confirm that the instrument is capable of measuring thermal conductivity of dense fluids with an accuracy of ± 1.0 percent. We expect to report results on helium, oxygen and propane in the near future.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://chemrxiv.org/doi/10.26434/chemrxiv.15002312\",\n      \"title\": \"Benchmarking Molecular Dynamics Simulations and Machine ...\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Benchmarking Molecular Dynamics Simulations and ...\\nby E Ogbomo · Cited by 1 — We compare the performance of two widely used computational approaches for the prediction of the density, viscosity, and thermal conductivity of\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://chemrxiv.org/doi/pdf/10.26434/chemrxiv.15002312\",\n      \"title\": \"[PDF] Benchmarking Molecular Dynamics Simulations and Machine ...\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"[PDF] Benchmarking Molecular Dynamics Simulations and Machine ...\\nWe compare the performance of two widely used computational approaches for the prediction of the density, viscosity, and thermal conductivity of\\nBenchmarking Molecular Dynamics Simulations and ...\\nby E Ogbomo — In this study, we compare the fidelity of MD simulations and ML models for the pre- diction of the density, viscosity, and thermal conductivity\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/jres/087/4/V87-4.pdf\",\n      \"title\": \"The Thermal Conductivity of Oxygen\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"precision 42o) of the oxygen measurements is between 0.5 and 0.8 percent for wire temperature transients\\nof 4 to 5 K, while the accuracy is estimated to be 1.5 percent\\nKey words: Hot wire; oxygen; pressure; temperature; thermal conductivity; transient.\\nferent experimental temperatures and densities. The\\n-N\\n0_2\\nE\\n20\\nGo z w a\\nTEMPERATURE, K\\nFIGURE 2. Region of the PVT surface covered by the present thermal\\nconductivity measurements.\\npressure, temperature, and applied power are measured\\ndirectly, the thermal conductivity and the associated\\nregression error are obtained through the data reduction\\npredicted and experimental <sup>values is found to be within</sup>\\nexperimental\\nerror, the experiment\\ngenerally being\\nlower. We may thus conclude <sup>that convection is absent.</sup>\\n5. Correlation of the Thermal\\nConductivity Surface\\nIt is generally accepted that <sup>the thermal conductivity</sup>\\npirical representation developed in the next section, eqs\\n(9-13), lies intermediate between [5] and [17] and ex-\\nhibits even less asymmetry toward the lower densities.\\nWe conclude that for this temperature the experimental\\nresults agree within experimental error with current\\ntheoretical predictions.\\n_Region 2, The Extended Critical Region._\"\n      ]\n    