ion/9b1d9d87/var/data/static/img/usa-icons/share.svg)\n    \n    ## PERMALINK\n    \n     ![Copy icon](https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/pd-medc-pmc-cloudpmc-viewer/production/9b1d9d87/var/data/static/img/usa-icons/content_copy.svg) Copy\n    \n\n![Open article navigation icon](https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/pd-medc-pmc-cloudpmc-viewer/production/9b1d9d87/var/data/static/img/usa-icons/list.svg)\n\nAs a library, NLM provides access to scientific literature. Inclusion in an NLM database does not imply endorsement of, or agreement with, the contents by NLM or the National Institutes of Health.  \nLearn more: [PMC Disclaimer](/about/disclaimer/) | [PMC Copyright Notice](/about/copyright/)\n\n![Royal Society of Chemistry logo](https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/banners/logo-rscsd.png)\n\nDigit Discov\n\n. 2026 Sep 29. Online ahead of print. doi: [10.1039/d6dd00260a](https://doi.org/10.1039/d6dd00260a)\n\n-   [Search in PMC](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/search/?term=\"Digit%20Discov\"[jour])\n-   [Search in PubMed](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term=\"Digit%20Discov\"[jour])\n-   [View in NLM Catalog](https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/nlmcatalog?term=\"Digit%20Discov\"[Title%20Abbreviation])\n-   [Add to search](?term=\"Digit%20Discov\"[jour])\n\n# Benchmarking machine learning and molecular dynamics methods for the prediction of the thermophysical properties of organic liquids\n\n[Egheosa Ogbomo](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term=\"Ogbomo%20E\"[Author])\n\n### Egheosa Ogbomo\n\naDepartment of Mechanical Engineering, Imperial College London, South Kensington Campus, London SW7 2AZ, UK, Email: fionn.carman18@imperial.ac.uk, Email: j.ewen@imperial.ac.uk\n\nFind articles by [Egheosa Ogbomo](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term=\"Ogbomo%20E\"[Author])\n\na,†, [Fionn Carman](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term=\"Carman%20F\"[Author])\n\n### Fionn Carman\n\naDepartment of Mechanical Engineering, Imperial College London, South Kensington Campus, London SW7 2AZ, UK, Email: fionn.carman18@imperial.ac.uk, Email: j.ewen@imperial.ac.uk\n\nFind articles by [Fionn Carman](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term=\"Carman%20F\"[Author])\n\na,†,✉, [Daniele Dini](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term=\"Dini%20D\"[Author])\n\n### Daniele Dini\n\naDepartment of Mechanical Engineering, Imperial College London, South Kensington Campus, London SW7 2AZ, UK, Email: fionn.carman18@imperial.ac.uk, Email: j.ewen@imperial.ac.uk\n\nFind articles by [Daniele Dini](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term=\"Dini%20D\"[Author])\n\na, [James P Ewen](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term=\"Ewen%20JP\"[Author])\n\n### James P Ewen\n\naDepartment of Mechanical Engineering, Imperial College London, South Kensington Campus, London SW7 2AZ, UK, Email: fionn.carman18@imperial.ac.uk, Email: j.ewen@imperial.ac.uk\n\nbDepartment of Mechanical Engineering, University of Bath, Claverton Down, Bath BA2 7AY, UK\n\nFind articles by [James P Ewen](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term=\"Ewen%20JP\"[Author])\n\na,b,✉\n\n-   Author information\n-   Article notes\n-   Copyright and License information\n\naDepartment of Mechanical Engineering, Imperial College London, South Kensington Campus, London SW7 2AZ, UK, Email: fionn.carman18@imperial.ac.uk, Email: j.ewen@imperial.ac.uk\n\nbDepartment of Mechanical Engineering, University of Bath, Claverton Down, Bath BA2 7AY, UK\n\n†\n\nThese authors contributed equally.\n\n✉\n\nCorresponding author.\n\nReceived 2026 May 5; Accepted 2026 Sep 18.\n\nThis journal is © The Royal Society of Chemistry\n\nThis article is licensed under a [Creative Commons Attribution 3.0 Unported Licence](https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0/). You can use material from this article in other publications without requesting further permissions from the RSC, provided that the correct acknowledgement is given.\n\n[PMC Copyright notice](/about/copyright/)\n\nPMCID: PMC13647254  PMID: [42852142](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/42852142/)\n\n## Abstract\n\nAccurate prediction of thermophysical properties is crucial for the rational design of lubricants and cooling fluids. We compile a database of approximately 4700 critically evaluated reference values for the density, viscosity, and thermal conductivity of 835 unique organic molecules in the C10–C50 range. We use this database to train and benchmark a range of machine learning (ML) architectures and molecular representations. XGBoost models trained on molecular descriptors are consistently among the most accurate across all three target properties. Out-of-distribution tests show that descriptor-space distance and the position of the predicted value within the training range identify higher-risk regions at the population level, rather than ordering the errors of individual molecules. Equilibrium molecular dynamics (EMD) simulations of eight representative molecules reproduce density accurately but show systematic, chemistry-dependent force-field errors for the viscosity and thermal conductivity. At an inference cost orders of magnitude lower than EMD simulations, the ML models are well suited to high-throughput screening of new candidate molecules for lubricants and other formulated products.\n\n---\n\nMachine learning models trained on ∼4700 reference values predict the density, viscosity, and thermal conductivity of lubricant-sized organic molecules, benchmarked against Green–Kubo molecular dynamics simulations of eight representative fluids.![graphic file with name d6dd00260a-ga.webp](https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/2340/13647254/db37733bedc7/d6dd00260a-ga.webp)\n\n## Introduction\n\nThermophysical properties such as the density, viscosity, and thermal conductivity of organic molecules are important to the performance of liquid formulations such as lubricants and refrigerants.[1](#cit1) Accurate prediction of these properties for pure organic molecules is also important for a wide range of industries, including energy, chemicals, and pharmaceuticals.[2–4](#cit2) In this context, the lack of reliable thermophysical data is one of the largest stumbling blocks in rational molecular,[5](#cit5) formulation,[6](#cit6) and process[7](#cit7) design. In particular, lubricants are required to adhere to strict viscosity limits across a wide temperature range (40–100 °C);[8](#cit8) however, for the organic molecules relevant to this application (C10–C50),[9](#cit9) viscosity–temperature data are still lacking. Viscosity data are available for many normal alkanes up to ∼C24, but data for branched alkanes, cycloalkanes, aromatics, alkyl cycloalkanes, and alkyl aromatics is much more sparse.[10](#cit10) There is also a lack of reliable thermophysical data available for oxygen-containing molecules such as esters, ethers, and glycols in the C10–C50 range.[11](#cit11) It is often possible to interpolate between missing entries with calculated predictions using a reliable equation of state, such as the perturbed-chain statistical associating fluid theory (PC-SAFT).[12,13](#cit12) Several other predictive models are available for pure hydrocarbons,[14](#cit14) with the most promising being group contribution and particularly entropy scaling methods.[15,16](#cit15) Quantitative structure–property relationship (QSPR) models[17–21](#cit17) have also been identified that are capable of predicting the viscosity of organic liquids. Within the chemical families and conditions for which they are parameterized, these methods can be highly accurate; their main limitation is reduced transferability, with predictive performance degrading outside the molecules and conditions for which experimental data are available.[14](#cit14) This makes extrapolation beyond the experimentally gathered dataset to new molecules or conditions less reliable. A quantitative comparison between these physically based methods and the machine learning models benchmarked here would require method-specific parameterization across the full dataset, and is left for future work.\n\nOver the last few decades, molecular dynamics (MD) simulations have become established as a useful tool with which to predict thermophysical properties and generate reliable datasets.[22](#cit22) MD simulations can in principle be applied to any organic liquid for which the molecular structure is known and a suitable force field is available, with an accuracy set by the force field and the sampling.[23,24](#cit23) The accuracy of force fields for MD simulations of the thermophysical properties of small molecules[25](#cit25) and linear and branched hydrocarbons has recently been studied.[26](#cit26) However, this type of comparison is still lacking for other industrially important classes of organic molecules, such as those containing cycloalkane, aromatic, ester, and ether groups. MD simulations are now routinely used to calculate the shear viscosity of single-component,[26–29](#cit26) binary mixtures,[30–34](#cit30) and multi-component[35–37](#cit35) hydrocarbon fluids with high accuracy over a wide range of conditions. In most cases, viscosity calculations through MD simulations have been restricted to small molecules, typically linear or branched alkanes. This is because larger hydrocarbons generally have higher viscosities,[38](#cit38) which are more challenging to accurately quantify through MD simulations due to their longer relaxation times.[39](#cit39) While MD simulations have also been used to predict the density and viscosity of oxygen-containing molecules[40,41](#cit40) and their mixtures,[42](#cit42) there are far fewer studies than for aliphatic hydrocarbons.\n\nMachine learning (ML) has also emerged as a useful tool for predicting the thermophysical properties of pure organic liquids[43](#cit43) and their mixtures.[44](#cit44) Accurate viscosity prediction has been achieved using ML models such as artificial neural networks for single-component,[45–51](#cit45) binary mixtures,[52](#cit52) and multi-component[53,54](#cit53) organic liquids. ML methods have also been shown to be effective in predicting the viscosity of more complex molecules, such as ionic liquids[55](#cit55) and deep eutectic solvents.[56](#cit56) As with QSPR models, the performance of ML models remains at the mercy of accurate training data availability, with small, sparse or noisy datasets all having adverse effects on model accuracy. Even the largest available datasets on the thermophysical properties of fluids, such as the NIST REFPROP database,[57](#cit57) are sparse and subject to considerable uncertainties compared to other popular applications of ML.[43](#cit43)\n\nSome recent studies have combined MD and ML by using dynamic descriptors from the MD simulations to improve ML predictions of various fluid properties.[58,59](#cit58) In other cases, MD simulations have been used to generate reliable datasets with which to train ML models that can then be used for fluid property prediction.[60,61](#cit60) MD simulations have also been used to generate large databases of viscosity values, which have then been used to optimize the molecular structure of lubricants with high viscosity index using ML methods.[5](#cit5) A combination of MD simulations and ML has also been used to facilitate the data-driven design of polymer-based viscosity index improver additives.[62](#cit62) A recent study compiled a large experimental database containing viscosity values for 6120 small organic liquids[59,63](#cit59) and used this to train ML models to predict viscosity.[64](#cit64) They found that the ML prediction models outperformed MD simulations across a range of molecules and temperatures.[64](#cit64) A combination of ML and MD simulations was recently employed to predict the density, kinematic viscosity, thermal conductivity, specific heat capacity, and dielectric constant of esters in the context of immersion cooling fluids.[65](#cit65) Both MD simulations[66](#cit66) and ML models[67](#cit67) have been considered for thermophysical property prediction for decades; however, only relatively recent advances in computational hardware and software have made this type of wide parameter study feasible.[22](#cit22) However, systematic side-by-side evaluations of the two approaches on common sets of fuel- and lubricant-sized organic molecules remain scarce.\n\nIn this study, we benchmark ML models for the prediction of the density, viscosity, and thermal conductivity of lubricant-relevant organic liquids, and use MD simulations on a representative subset as an independent, physics-based assessment of the same properties. The primary aim is to develop ML models suitable for high-throughput virtual screening[62,68](#cit62) and molecular discovery[5,69](#cit5) workflows for high-performance lubricants. The MD simulations serve a complementary purpose, probing where and why physics-based prediction succeeds or fails for these types of molecules.\n\nWe evaluate 26 combinations of model architecture and molecular representation on approximately 4700 critically evaluated reference values for 835 fuel and lubricant-sized molecules, to identify the most accurate combination for each property. Because screening is most valuable beyond the chemistries that have already been measured, we then examine how far that accuracy extends to molecules unlike those seen in training, and which signals available at prediction time indicate where it does not. Finally, we apply Green–Kubo MD simulations to eight molecules, two from each of the four principal chemical families of the dataset, at 40 °C and 100 °C. This extends force field benchmarks that have concentrated on linear and branched alkanes[26,28,29](#cit26) to alkyl cycloalkanes, alkyl aromatics, esters, and ethers, for which experimental viscosity data remain sparse[10](#cit10) despite their importance in automotive fuels,[70](#cit70) aviation fuels,[71](#cit71) and lubricants.[9,72](#cit9)\n\n## Methodology\n\n### Machine learning models\n\nTo construct a robust and diverse dataset for model development and evaluation, we collected reference data for density, thermal conductivity and dynamic viscosity at atmospheric pressure and at both 40 °C and 100 °C. Only molecules with a dynamic viscosity of approximately 1–50 mPa s at 40 °C were included, because this is the range accessible to equilibrium molecular dynamics (EMD) simulations.[39](#cit39) Data were compiled from the Landolt–Börnstein series[73](#cit73) and the NIST Chemistry WebBook[74](#cit74) (accessed June 2024), resulting in 835 unique molecules carrying approximately 4700 critically evaluated reference values after the structure canonicalization, unit conversion and deduplication steps described below.\n\nIn cases where the properties of interest were not provided exactly at 40 °C or 100 °C, interpolation was used to obtain the values at these temperatures. The NIST WebBook[74](#cit74) utilizes a combination of property estimation methods to interpolate liquid viscosity values from the available experimental data. These include the PPDS9 (Physical Property Data Service) and ViscosityL correlations, which are based on semi-empirical and theoretical equations of state (extended Andrade models[75,76](#cit75)) fitted from experimental values.[4](#cit4) The source method of every label is recorded, and the number of labels obtained by direct measurement *versus* by each database's own correlation or reference–property model is reported by property and temperature in Table S2. Most of the labels for density and thermal conductivity (99.0% and 97.8% at 40 °C) and approximately 44% of the viscosity labels are values produced by the source databases' own correlation, interpolation or reference–property models rather than direct measurements. The errors reported in this work therefore measure agreement with these adopted reference values, and do not include the uncertainty of the underlying database correlations. For molecules where both a direct measurement and a correlation value are available, the two agree closely across the full viscosity range at both temperatures (Fig. S1).\n\nFor interpolation of the liquid density values, the NIST WebBook uses the Tait equation.[77](#cit77) For liquid thermal conductivity, the TransportPolynomial model was applied, utilizing temperature-dependent polynomial fits to experimental thermal conductivity data. For the Landolt–Börnstein series,[73](#cit73) cubic spline interpolation using a minimum of three surrounding data points was employed to estimate the property values at the desired temperatures. In cases where only the kinematic viscosity was reported, it was converted to dynamic viscosity using *µ* = *ν* × *ρ*, where *µ* is dynamic viscosity, *ν* is kinematic viscosity, and *ρ* is density. Molecules whose viscosity was reported only as kinematic and for which no density was available at the same temperature were excluded (283 molecules at each temperature). Canonicalization and deduplication of the 1187 source entries give 1114 unique molecules, of which 835 retain at least one usable label and form the dataset used here. The viscosity values were normalized by taking their natural logarithm to improve regression performance during ML training.[54](#cit54)\n\nA summary of the reference dataset is presented in [Table 1](#tab1). Of the 835 unique molecules, 637 (76%) are pure hydrocarbons containing only carbon and hydrogen, while the remaining 198 (24%) are oxygen-containing organic compounds such as esters and ethers. Oxygen-containing molecules were included alongside the pure hydrocarbons because oxygenated lubricant-like molecules, such as synthetic esters,[78](#cit78) are an important part of the target application space, and both classes are represented in the MD benchmark below. The chemical family composition of the reference dataset is summarized in Table S1, with each molecule assigned to a single chemical family, as recorded in the released dataset. Some molecules may belong to more than one family due to the presence of multiple functional groups.\n\n#### Table 1. Summary of the critically evaluated reference dataset compiled from the Landolt–Börnstein series[73](#cit73) and NIST Chemistry WebBook.[74](#cit74) The dataset contains thermophysical properties for 835 unique organic molecules.\n\n   \n\nProperty\n\nNo. of compounds\n\nMin. value[a](#tab1fna)\n\nMax. value[a](#tab1fna)\n\nDensity (40 °C)\n\n770\n\n0.715\n\n1.400\n\nDensity (100 °C)\n\n769\n\n0.668\n\n1.340\n\nDynamic viscosity (40 °C)\n\n799\n\n0.54\n\n56.30\n\nDynamic viscosity (100 °C)\n\n804\n\n0.29\n\n22.00\n\nThermal conductivity (40 °C)\n\n785\n\n0.054\n\n0.276\n\nThermal conductivity (100 °C)\n\n786\n\n0.051\n\n0.250\n\n[Open in a new tab](table/tab1/)\n\na\n\nUnits for density, viscosity and thermal conductivity are g cm−3, mPa s and W m−1 K−1, respectively.\n\nIn this study, we compare the most commonly used molecular representations on our reference dataset to see which enables ML models to perform best on transport property prediction tasks. [Fig. 1](#fig1) shows the different molecular representations used to train ML models in this study.\n\n#### Fig. 1. The molecular representations used to train the ML models, illustrated for butylbenzene 138%.\n\n![Fig. 1](https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/2340/13647254/a84aa892b351/d6dd00260a-f1.webp)\n\n[Open in a new tab](figure/fig1/)\n\nMolecular descriptors are numerical features that encode chemical, physical, and structural properties of molecules and are widely used for ML-based property prediction.[79,80](#cit79) In this study, descriptors were generated using the RDKit and Mordred Python libraries,[80](#cit80) yielding 1019 descriptors per molecule after removal of invalid, missing, and categorical features. Descriptor scaling was applied selectively based on model requirements. *Z*\\-score normalization was used for magnitude-sensitive models (SVM and *k*\\-NN), while no scaling was applied for tree-based models (random forest and XGBoost) to preserve the natural feature distributions and chemically meaningful thresholds.\n\nDimensionality-reduced embeddings provide a compact alternative to high-dimensional molecular descriptors by reducing noise and redundancy while retaining the most informative features.[81](#cit81) In this study, embeddings generated using principal component analysis (PCA) and uniform manifold approximation and projection (UMAP) were evaluated across a range of dimensionalities to assess their impact on model performance, as both the choice of method and retained dimensionality can influence predictive accuracy. PCA and UMAP embeddings were used as inputs to ML models to compare linear and nonlinear dimensionality reduction strategies for molecular property prediction. A detailed analysis of embedding method and dimensionality effects, including root-mean-square error (RMSE) trends across properties and model types, is provided in the SI. Embeddings were generated using the scikit-learn and UMAP-learn libraries from *z*\\-score normalized molecular descriptors.\n\nMolecular fingerprints are typically represented as 2D binary strings, where each bit indicates the presence or absence of a specific structural feature or substructure within a molecule. A widely used and generally high-performing fingerprint type is the extended-connectivity fingerprint (ECFP), with the Morgan fingerprint being a common example.[82](#cit82) In this study, ECFPs are generated using the Morgan Fingerprint implementation in RDKit.[79](#cit79) While prior work has examined optimal ECFP parameters for molecular similarity assessment,[83](#cit83) no studies have specifically focused on optimizing ECFP generation for the property prediction of fuel- and lubricant-sized organic molecules using ML models. Accordingly, this study evaluates a range of ECFP generation parameters to identify those that yield the best regression performance. A systematic analysis of fingerprint parameters, including radius, bit size, and hashed *versus* unhashed representations,[83](#cit83) is provided in the SI.\n\nMolecular graphs encode information about molecules by representing atoms as nodes and the bonds between them as edges. Nodes can be characterized by atom-level features such as atomic number, while the edges can incorporate bond-related characteristics such as bond type. Table S3 shows the features used to create molecular graphs in this study.\n\nSimplified molecular-input line-entry system (SMILES) strings encode molecular structures as sequences of ASCII characters[84](#cit84) but are not inherently unique, as multiple valid strings can represent the same molecule. Canonical SMILES address this by enforcing a deterministic representation,[85](#cit85) although models trained on canonical SMILES may overfit to syntactic patterns rather than underlying chemistry.[86](#cit86) Despite this, SMILES remain widely used due to their simplicity and compatibility with sequence-based models such as CNNs and transformers.[87](#cit87) SELFIES (Self-Referencing Embedded Strings) were introduced to guarantee syntactic validity, ensuring that any token sequence corresponds to a chemically valid molecule.[88](#cit88) In this study, both canonical SMILES and SELFIES were evaluated as input representations for molecular property prediction. Strings were converted to token embeddings prior to model training; SMILES were generated from IUPAC names,[89](#cit89) and SELFIES were generated using the published SELFIES API.[88](#cit88)\n\nPretrained transformer embeddings were obtained from ChemBERTa,[90,91](#cit90) a chemistry language model pretrained on large SMILES corpora. The frozen ChemBERTa-77M-MTR encoder was used as a fixed feature extractor. Each molecule's SMILES string is passed through the pretrained encoder and the token representations are mean-pooled (weighted by the attention mask) into a single fixed-length vector, with no fine-tuning of the encoder weights. These frozen embeddings were used as inputs to a multilayer perceptron (MLP) head and to an XGBoost model, providing a transfer-learning representation that complements the descriptor-, fingerprint-, and string-based inputs above.\n\nIn this study, we evaluate a range of models using the collated dataset to identify the most suitable algorithm for property prediction. The classic ML models, including support vector machines (SVMs),[92](#cit92) random forests (RFs),[93](#cit93) and *k*\\-nearest neighbors (*k*\\-NN),[94](#cit94) are implemented using the scikit-learn package in Python.[95](#cit95) XGBoost (Extreme Gradient Boosting) models were trained using the XGBoost Python library.[96](#cit96) The graph-based models, message passing neural networks (MPNNs)[97](#cit97) and graph attention networks (GATs),[98](#cit98) are implemented with the PyTorch Geometric library.[99](#cit99) The deep learning models, convolutional neural networks (CNNs),[100](#cit100) multilayer perceptrons,[101](#cit101) and transformers[87](#cit87) were implemented with the PyTorch library.[99](#cit99) ChemBERTa[90,91](#cit90) was evaluated in two configurations using the HuggingFace Transformers library: fine-tuning the full pretrained encoder end-to-end on SMILES, and the frozen-embedding configuration described above, in which only a small MLP head is trained on the fixed mean-pooled encoder output.\n\nAll models were evaluated under a single shared nested cross-validation protocol, applied to molecular representation configurations that were fixed in advance. The embedding dimensionality and the fingerprint radius and bit size were chosen by grid search on a single 80 : 10 : 10 train/validation/test split, as reported in the SI, so the protocol is nested with respect to hyperparameter selection and conditional on those representation configurations. The dataset was partitioned into five fixed outer folds, so that both temperatures of a given molecule always fall in the same fold and no molecule appears in more than one outer test set. The folds were constructed by stratified sampling over quintiles of the log-transformed 40 °C viscosity labels (molecules without a viscosity label were assigned *via* median-filled strata), then frozen. For each outer fold, hyperparameters were selected by random search over 25 configurations sampled from model-specific ranges, scored on a designated validation partition of the non-test data (approximately 85 : 15 train/validation); the selected configuration was then retrained on all non-test data and evaluated once on the untouched outer test fold. Full details of the architectures, hyperparameter ranges, and training procedures for each model are provided in the SI.\n\n### Molecular dynamics simulations\n\nThe molecular structures of the eight molecules selected for this benchmarking study are shown in [Fig. 2](#fig2). These molecules were chosen to span the principal chemical families encountered in the reference dataset used in this work. All the MD simulations were performed using the LAMMPS software.[102](#cit102) EMD simulations were employed to calculate the density, viscosity, and thermal conductivity of the eight target molecules at 40 °C and 100 °C, consistent with conditions used to determine lubricant viscosity index values.[8](#cit8) All simulations were performed using the LOPLS-AA force field.[103](#cit103) Parameters for the LOPLS simulations were taken from Jorgensen *et al.*[104](#cit104) and Siu *et al.*[103](#cit103) Where required, the ester extension to LOPLS-AA was employed,[105](#cit105) while aromatic and ether parameters were taken from the corresponding OPLS-AA parameterizations. LOPLS-AA was selected because previous benchmarks have demonstrated it to be among the most accurate all-atom force fields for reproducing the experimental density and viscosity of linear hydrocarbons, including *n*\\-hexadecane,[28](#cit28)*n*\\-dodecane, and *n*\\-octacosane.[29](#cit29)\n\n#### Fig. 2. The eight MD target molecules, two from each principal chemical family of lubricant base oils in the dataset: alkanes (squalane, nonylcyclohexane), alkyl aromatics (4-hexyl-1,7-diphenylheptane, 2-(1-propylheptyl)naphthalene), esters (decyl stearate, bis(2-ethylhexyl) sebacate), and polyethers (7,10,13-trioxanonadecane, tetraethylene glycol diethyl ether).\n\n![Fig. 2](https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/2340/13647254/5c1a04b60106/d6dd00260a-f2.webp)\n\n[Open in a new tab](figure/fig2/)\n\nInitial single molecule geometries were generated from the SMILES strings in RDKit by adding explicit hydrogens, embedding with the ETKDGv3 distance geometry algorithm,[106](#cit106) and minimizing with UFF. Cubic starting configurations were assembled with Packmol[107](#cit107) at a low initial density (0.3 g cm−3), with molecule counts selected to give approximately 15 000 atoms per box (163–349 molecules, depending on molecular size), corresponding to equilibrated cubic box lengths of 5.2–5.5 nm. A dedicated system size sensitivity study was not performed here, since all systems are well above the range where finite-size effects would be expected to affect the viscosity[39](#cit39) and thermal conductivity.[108](#cit108) Periodic boundary conditions were applied in all three Cartesian directions.\n\nThe protocol suggested by Schmitt *et al.*[26](#cit26) was adopted in the EMD simulations because it has been shown to enable accurate calculation of the viscosity of lubricant-sized molecules. A velocity–Verlet integrator with a 1.0 fs timestep was employed.[109](#cit109) Bonds containing hydrogen atoms were constrained with the SHAKE algorithm.[110](#cit110) Constraining C–H bonds is important for obtaining accurate thermal conductivities with all-atom force fields, as it suppresses the classically over-excited high-frequency C–H stretching modes that otherwise inflate the Green–Kubo heat flux.[111](#cit111) Lennard-Jones interactions were truncated at 12 Å with long-range tail corrections applied to energy and pressure, and long-range electrostatics were treated using the particle–particle particle–mesh (PPPM) method[112](#cit112) with a relative accuracy of 10−5. Geometric mixing rules were used for the unlike-atom interactions, and intramolecular 1–4 interactions were scaled by 0.5 following the OPLS convention.\n\nThe system was first relaxed by conjugate gradient energy minimization, followed by a short displacement-capped Langevin dynamics stage to remove any remaining close contacts. An equilibration in the canonical (*NVT*) ensemble was then performed at the target temperature to allow the system to reach thermal equilibrium. The density was then equilibrated at 1.0 atm and the target temperature for 1.0 ns in the isothermal–isobaric (*NPT*) ensemble using the Nosé–Hoover thermostat and barostat[113,114](#cit113) with damping coefficients of 100 fs and 1000 fs, respectively. The density was obtained directly as the ensemble averaged mass density at atmospheric pressure over the final 0.25 ns of the *NPT* trajectory, where a steady-state value had been reached.[26](#cit26) Density uncertainties were estimated as five-block standard deviations over this averaging window and are at most 0.003 g cm−3 (≤0.4%) for every state point. The simulation box was then deformed isotropically over 0.5 ns at constant temperature until the fluid density matched this average value. For each molecule, 10 independent replicas were branched from the equilibrated configuration with independently seeded velocities, each given a 0.2 ns *NVT* warm-up before production. Production was carried out in the *NVT* ensemble for 8 ns per replica to allow the slow shear-stress autocorrelation tail to converge.[26](#cit26)\n\nViscosity was calculated using the Green–Kubo (GK) formalism,[115,116](#cit115) which is well suited to low and moderate viscosity fluids (<50 mPa s).[39](#cit39) The GK expression for the shear viscosity is:\n\n![graphic file with name d6dd00260a-t1.webp](https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/2340/13647254/b5e713c20886/d6dd00260a-t1.webp)\n\n1\n\nwhere *η* is the viscosity, *V* is the system volume, *k*B is the Boltzmann constant, *T* is the temperature, and *σ**αβ*(*t*) is an off-diagonal component of the stress tensor at time *t* (with *α* and *β* denoting Cartesian directions *x*, *y*, or *z*); the angle brackets denote the stress autocorrelation function (ACF) averaged over time origins. To maximize the statistical sampling, the viscosity was averaged over all six independent components of the pressure tensor, combining the three off-diagonal terms with the three diagonal-difference terms, as recommended by Maginn *et al.*[39](#cit39)\n\nThe time decomposition method (TDM) of Zhang *et al.*[117](#cit117) was used to improve the reliability of the calculations for the high-viscosity molecules. The time evolution of the running integral *η*(*t*) was recorded over the full production trajectory of each replica, with the stress ACF evaluated using a fast Fourier transform (FFT) technique.[118](#cit118) A cutoff time *t*cut was applied to the integral to mitigate accumulation of long-time noise, determined from the ratio of the standard deviation *σ**η*(*t*) across replicas to the average running viscosity:[117](#cit117)![graphic file with name d6dd00260a-t2.webp](https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/2340/13647254/4a291c95e0ca/d6dd00260a-t2.webp)matching the criterion of Schmitt *et al.*,[26](#cit26) who showed that ratios of *σ**η*/*η*final between 0.2 and 0.8 do not materially affect the extracted values. The bootstrapping method,[119](#cit119) with 200 iterations, was used to compute the final viscosity value and its uncertainty, following the time-decomposition procedure of the PyLaT software[120](#cit120) and implemented in the released analysis script. Random combinations of the 10 replicas were resampled with replacement, and for each combination the replica-averaged running viscosity *η̄*(*t*) was fitted with the double-exponential function of [eqn (2)](#eqn2), in which *η*∞ is the long-time viscosity limit and *α*, *β*1, and *β*2 are fitting parameters:\n\n![graphic file with name d6dd00260a-t3.webp](https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/2340/13647254/cff947f0fa90/d6dd00260a-t3.webp)\n\n2\n\nThis functional form reflects the physics of the underlying stress autocorrelation function, which decays through two distinct regimes: a fast relaxation associated with local, high-frequency stress fluctuations and a slow relaxation governed by collective, long-time structural rearrangement.[117](#cit117) The two exponential terms, with time constants *β*1 and *β*2 and relative weight *α*, capture these two modes, so that the running integral *η*(*t*) rises from zero and saturates toward the plateau value *η*∞, which is taken as the converged viscosity. Fitting to this form allows the well-sampled short-time behavior to determine *η*∞ while avoiding the accumulation of statistical noise in the poorly converged long-time tail of the integral, thereby providing a more robust estimate than reading off the running integral directly.[39,117](#cit39)\n\nThe Green–Kubo formalism was also used to compute the thermal conductivity *κ*,[121,122](#cit121) from the autocorrelation of the microscopic heat flux vector **J**q:\n\n![graphic file with name d6dd00260a-t4.webp](https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/2340/13647254/c854af44b33a/d6dd00260a-t4.webp)\n\n3\n\nwhere *κ* is the thermal conductivity, **J**q is the heat flux vector, the factor of three averages over the Cartesian components of the heat flux, and the angle brackets indicate a time-origin average of the autocorrelation function. The microscopic heat current was evaluated in LAMMPS using the centroid-based per-atom stress formulation of Surblys *et al.*[123](#cit123) for the pair, bond, angle, dihedral, and improper virial contributions. This formulation treats the many-body bonded interactions consistently in the heat-current expression, which is essential for obtaining accurate thermal conductivities in molecular systems with angle, dihedral, and improper terms.\n\nAs with the viscosity, the thermal conductivity was obtained from the running Green–Kubo integral *κ*(*t*), with the heat-flux ACF averaged over the three Cartesian components and evaluated by FFT.[118](#cit118) Unlike the shear stress, however, the heat-flux ACF carries no slow tail. The ACF decays to zero within a few picoseconds, so the running integral *κ*(*t*) reaches an early plateau and then drifts as statistical noise accumulates in the long-time tail. The double-exponential TDM fit used for the viscosity is therefore not required for the thermal conductivity, and integrating *κ*(*t*) to long lag times would only accumulate noise; the uncertainty of the Green–Kubo estimate grows with the upper integration limit relative to the total trajectory length,[39](#cit39) so the conductivity must be read from the short-time plateau rather than the converged integral. To locate this plateau without a hand-chosen cutoff, we first identified the lag time *t*\\* at which the normalized heat-flux ACF decays into its noise floor, defined as the first point beyond which its magnitude remains below twice the standard deviation of the long-time, noise-dominated tail. The thermal conductivity was then taken as the average of *κ*(*t*) over the window \\[*t*\\*, *t*\\* + 10 ps\\], immediately after the autocorrelation has decayed and before long-time noise sets in, with the reported value and uncertainty given by the mean and standard deviation of this window average across the 10 independent replicas.\n\n## Results and discussion\n\n### Comparing ML models\n\nPrior to benchmarking, we investigated embedding and fingerprinting strategies by systematic grid search on a single 80 : 10 : 10 train/validation/test split, with full details provided in the SI (Fig. S2–S5, Tables S4 and S5). For embeddings, PCA consistently outperformed UMAP across most properties and model types; one hypothesis is that its linear variance-preserving transformations suit the axis-aligned splits used by tree-based models, though the benchmark does not test this directly. Based on these results, PCA embeddings of 100 dimensions were used for all models. For fingerprints, increasing radius and bit size improved performance despite higher bit-collision rates, and unhashed Morgan fingerprints with a radius of 8 were selected as they eliminated collision-induced ambiguity and yielded the best overall predictive performance. Both comparisons rest on that single split and were not repeated within each outer fold, so they rank embedding and fingerprint configurations under one split rather than under the nested protocol.\n\nAll 26 model–representation combinations were then evaluated under the shared nested cross-validation protocol described in the Methods, using the same fixed outer folds for every model, representation and property. Conditional on the representation configurations fixed above, the quoted test errors are therefore free of test-set-tuned hyperparameter selection, although the outer folds serve as a comparative evaluation across the 26 combinations rather than a fully untouched holdout for the single winning model. All metrics reported below are means over the five outer folds, with the between-fold standard deviation as an estimate of their variability.\n\nTable S6 reports the mean test RMSE and *R*2 for every model–representation combination, property and temperature; per-fold metrics, including mean absolute error (MAE) and the between-fold standard deviations, are provided with the released code. To identify the strongest models we *z*\\-score normalize the per-property RMSE values, invert them so that lower error corresponds to higher performance, and average across properties; we use this ranking only to select a common set of leading models and base all substantive claims on the property-specific results.\n\n[Fig. 3](#fig3) shows how the five best-ranked models perform for each property at 40 °C (a–c) and 100 °C (d–f). Here “PCA embeddings” refers to the 100-dimensional PCA of molecular descriptors described above, whereas “ChemBERTa embeddings” refers to the fixed mean-pooled vectors produced by the pretrained ChemBERTa encoder.[90,91](#cit90)\n\n#### Fig. 3. The five best-ranked ML model–representation combinations for each property at 40 °C (a–c) and 100 °C (d–f): (a and d) viscosity, modeled as ln(*η*/mPa s), (b and e) density and (c and f) thermal conductivity. Bars are mean test RMSE over the five locked outer folds, ordered by rank, with between-fold standard deviations as error bars. “PCA embeddings” denotes the 100-dimensional PCA of molecular descriptors and “ChemBERTa embeddings” the frozen mean-pooled ChemBERTa vectors. Results for all 26 combinations are given in Table S6.\n\n![Fig. 3](https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/2340/13647254/0cb396b335f7/d6dd00260a-f3.webp)\n\n[Open in a new tab](figure/fig3/)\n\nXGBoost and RF models, when used with molecular descriptors, performed most consistently across all three transport properties. XGBoost with descriptors gave the lowest viscosity error of any combination (RMSE = 0.35 and 0.30 in ln(*η*/mPa s) at 40 and 100 °C, *R*2 = 0.86 and 0.83) and was among the best two or three combinations for density and thermal conductivity, while RF with descriptors gave the lowest density error (RMSE = 0.047 g cm−3). An error of 0.35 on the logarithmic scale corresponds to a typical discrepancy of a factor of 1.4 between predicted and measured viscosity. This aligns with previous studies,[124,125](#cit124) which also found tree-based models to be reliable across datasets. These methods handle nonlinear relationships and high-dimensional features effectively, making them suitable for the diverse information captured by molecular descriptors, and are relatively insensitive to feature scale, irregular target functions and uninformative features.[126](#cit126)\n\nWhen comparing representations for XGBoost, overall predictive performance followed the trend descriptors > PCA embeddings > ChemBERTa embeddings > fingerprints, and this ordering is particularly pronounced for viscosity, where the fingerprint model was substantially weaker (RMSE = 0.67 and 0.55; *R*2 = 0.51 and 0.42). This may be explained by the fact that while descriptors and PCA embeddings can incorporate both structural and physicochemical information, fingerprints are purely structural, lacking explicit encoding of global molecular properties, which limits their expressiveness for the transport properties considered here.\n\nThe SVM and *k*\\-NN models with molecular descriptors also achieved accuracy close to the leading tree-based models. SVM with descriptors reached a viscosity RMSE of 0.38 and 0.32 (*R*2 = 0.84 and 0.80), while *k*\\-NN with descriptors was clearly weaker at 0.44 and 0.36. For these distance-based models, descriptor and PCA embedding inputs gave comparable accuracy (for example, SVM viscosity RMSE of 0.38/0.32 with descriptors *versus* 0.38/0.31 with PCA embeddings). Some sensitivity to the high dimensionality and redundancy of descriptor sets is expected for distance-based methods,[127](#cit127) and may explain their larger errors relative to the tree-based models.\n\nThe GAT and MPNN models, trained on molecular graphs augmented with three-dimensional geometric information (bond lengths and partial charges derived from ETKDG conformers), were the weakest combinations, with GAT giving a viscosity RMSE of 0.78 and 0.63 (*R*2 = 0.33 and 0.22). This is notable given the theoretical advantages of geometric information for modeling molecular properties, and that graph ML methods have shown strong performance on related tasks.[52,128](#cit52) Two factors plausibly contribute: graph models typically require larger datasets than the one used here,[129](#cit129) and ETKDG conformers,[106](#cit106) while inexpensive, do not always capture the most energetically favorable geometry of flexible molecules, so the bond lengths and partial charges may only approximate the environments that govern transport properties. More accurate conformer generation, using density functional theory or machine-learning force fields, is therefore one route to improvement. Within this study the MPNN outperformed the GAT for every property and temperature, by 19 and 26% in viscosity RMSE at 40 and 100 °C and by 4 to 15% for density and thermal conductivity, on identical graphs and under the same search budget, although both remained well behind the descriptor-based models.\n\nAmong the deep-learning models, the transformer trained on the PCA molecular embeddings fell in the lower third of the benchmark (*η* RMSE 0.53 and 0.39; *R*2 = 0.69 and 0.70), well behind the MLP trained on the same embeddings (0.43 and 0.37), so the shortfall lies with the architecture rather than the representation. The two ChemBERTa[91](#cit91) configurations behaved very differently. Used as a frozen feature extractor, with only a small MLP head trained on the fixed mean-pooled embedding of each molecule, ChemBERTa was one of the strongest models overall. It gave the lowest thermal-conductivity error at 40 °C (RMSE = 0.0095 W m−1 K−1, *R*2 = 0.89) and consistent performance across all three properties, with viscosity accuracy (RMSE = 0.41 and 0.34) moderately behind the leading descriptor models. By contrast, fine-tuning the full ChemBERTa encoder end-to-end on SMILES predicted viscosity somewhat more accurately (RMSE = 0.37 and 0.31) but was markedly less consistent, with substantially larger density and thermal-conductivity errors (*ρ R*2 = 0.51). This pattern suggests that, on a dataset of this size, updating all encoder weights can overwrite useful pretrained features, whereas freezing them and learning only a lightweight head preserves the transferable representation and generalizes better.\n\nA chemistry-pretrained transformer was thus among the best models for these transport properties when its pretrained embeddings were used directly. The comparatively poor SMILES fine-tuning is consistent with a distributional mismatch between ChemBERTa's predominantly drug-like pretraining corpus[91,130](#cit91) and the lubricant-sized molecules studied here, and points to parameter-efficient fine-tuning such as Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA),[131](#cit131) which freezes the base weights and introduces small trainable matrices, as a promising alternative, as demonstrated for other large chemistry and protein language models.[132](#cit132)\n\n\n...128565 bytes truncated...\n\nThe tool call succeeded but the output was truncated. Full output saved to: /home/hal/.local/share/opencode/tool-output/tool_14d33209d0012yhHOKameSB0G0\nUse Grep to search the full content or Read with offset/limit to view specific sections."
