    "content": "{\n  \"search_id\": \"search_4f12053d46c571cef94a744787521b4b\",\n  \"results\": [\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13647254\",\n      \"title\": \"Benchmarking machine learning and molecular dynamics methods for the prediction of the thermophysical properties of organic liquids\",\n      \"publish_date\": \"2026-09-29\",\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Accurate prediction of thermophysical properties is crucial for the rational design of lubricants and cooling fluids. We compile a database of approximately 4700 critically evaluated reference values for the density, viscosity, and thermal conductivity of 835 unique organic molecules in the C10–C50 range.\\nToc\\nMachine learning models trained on ∼4700 reference values predict the density, viscosity, and thermal conductivity of lubricant-sized organic molecules, benchmarked against Green–Kubo molecular dynamics simulations of eight representative fluids.\\nIntroduction\\nViscosity data are available for many normal alkanes up to ∼C24, but data for branched alkanes, cycloalkanes, aromatics, alkyl cycloalkanes, and alkyl aromatics is much more sparse.^10 There is also a lack of reliable thermophysical data available for oxygen-containing molecules such as esters, ethers, and glycols in the C10–C50 range.^11 It is\\nOf the 835 unique molecules, 637 (76%) are pure hydrocarbons containing only carbon and hydrogen, while the remaining 198 (24%) are oxygen-containing organic compounds such as esters and ethers.\\nOxygen-containing molecules were included alongside the pure hydrocarbons because oxygenated lubricant-like molecules, such as synthetic esters,^78 are an important part of the target application space, and both classes are represented in the MD benchmark below.\\nThe exception is tetraethylene glycol diethyl ether, the most oxygen-rich molecule of the set, which MD under-predicts by 10% at 40 °C and 13% at 100 °C.\\nSqualane deviates by −4% at 40 °C and −7% at 100 °C, nonylcyclohexane by +8% and −1%, 2-(1-propylheptyl)naphthalene by +12% and +14%, while 4-hexyl-1,7-diphenylheptane, under-predicted by 25% at 40 °C, recovers to −5% at 100 °C. By contrast, viscosity is under-predicted for all oxygen-containing molecules at both temperatures.\\nFour of the eight molecules lie within 25% of the reference values at 40 °C, with squalane under-predicted by 42% and decyl stearate, 4-hexyl-1,7-diphenylheptane, and bis(2-ethylhexyl) sebacate over-predicted by 44, 69, and 137%; at 100 °C four lie within 10%, with decyl stearate (+45%), 7,10,13-trioxanonadecane (−27%), tetraethylene glycol\\nTheir accuracy for viscosity and thermal conductivity is consequently uneven and chemistry-dependent. Viscosity is under-predicted for every oxygen-containing molecule, and thermal conductivity over-predicted proportionally with the aliphatic hydrogen content.\\nViscosity errors are small for the pure hydrocarbons with LOPLS, but this property is consistently under-predicted for the oxygen-containing molecules; the reference ranking of the fluids is nonetheless largely preserved.\\nThermal conductivity is over-predicted for every molecule, by amounts that correlate with the density of aliphatic C–H bonds, compressing the reference range and misordering the fluids.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/42852142\",\n      \"title\": \"Benchmarking machine learning and molecular dynamics methods for the prediction of the thermophysical properties of organic liquids.\",\n      \"publish_date\": \"2026-09-29\",\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Benchmarking machine learning and molecular dynamics methods for the prediction of the thermophysical properties of organic liquids.\\nAuthors: Ogbomo Egheosa, Carman Fionn, Dini Daniele, Ewen James P\\nJournal: Digital discovery. 2026.\\nPublished: 2026-09-29\\nPublication types: Journal Article\\nPMID: 42852142; DOI: 10.1039/d6dd00260a; PMCID: PMC13647254\\nAccurate prediction of thermophysical properties is crucial for the rational design of lubricants and cooling fluids. We compile a database of approximately 4700 critically evaluated reference values for the density, viscosity, and thermal conductivity of 835 unique organic molecules in the C10-C50 range.\\nEquilibrium molecular dynamics (EMD) simulations of eight representative molecules reproduce density accurately but show systematic, chemistry-dependent force-field errors for the viscosity and thermal conductivity.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://chemrxiv.org/doi/pdf/10.26434/chemrxiv.15002312\",\n      \"title\": \"[PDF] Benchmarking Molecular Dynamics Simulations and Machine ...\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"[PDF] Benchmarking Molecular Dynamics Simulations and Machine ...\\nWe explore the accuracy of the ML models in predicting the viscosity of molecules that were chemically distinct from those in the original\\nBenchmarking Molecular Dynamics Simulations and ...\\nby E Ogbomo — In this study, we compare the fidelity of MD simulations and ML models for the pre- diction of the density, viscosity, and thermal conductivity\\nBenchmarking Molecular Dynamics Simulations and Machine ...\\nAccurate prediction of the thermophysical properties of hydrocarbons is critical to the rational design of fuels, lubricants, and cooling fluids.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://ntrs.nasa.gov/api/citations/19740023269/downloads/19740023269.pdf\",\n      \"title\": \"VISCOSITY AND THERMAL CONDUCTIVITY COEFFICIENTS OF GASEOUS AND LIQUID OXYGEN by H. J. M. Hanley, R. D. McCarty, and J.\",\n      \"publish_date\": \"2008-03-27\",\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Viscosity of compressed oxygen (r\\\\ in milligram/cm.s) ... 38\\nV. Thermal conductivity of compressed oxygen\\n(A in milliwatt/m.K) 40\\nVI. Thermal conductivity of oxygen in the critical region\\n(A in milliwatt/m.K) 42\\nVII. Viscosity and thermal conductivity o