s predicted answer to the question. Under \"what the plan will ... predict needs no source\" → SUPPORTED.\n\nHmm!!! Then almost everything is SUPPORTED. That can't be the design... unless the design is exactly to test whether the checker wrongly demands sources for predictions. Hmm.\n\nBut the hypothesis carries \"near 25 percent\" — \"any value it carries against the page\". The value check: is 25% on the page? The page says \"Four of the eight molecules lie within 25% of the reference values at 40 °C\" for viscosity. So 25% appears on the page but with different meaning (4/8 within 25%), and errors for others are 42–137%. An \"error floor near 25 percent\" is not stated. So the value check fails → PARTIAL at best.\n\nI think hypothesis → PARTIAL is the best-judgment answer: the hypothesis is the plan's conjecture, but it asserts a quantitative error floor (~25%) and irreducibility that the cited source does not state; the source supports only the qualitative chemistry-dependent error claim. PARTIAL.\n\nFor consistency, why not also PARTIAL for if_lever/if_not (thresholds not in source)? Because those are explicitly conditional predictions of the plan's own experiment (if_lever/if_not branches), while the hypothesis asserts a property of the method in general present tense (\"has a ... error floor\"). Hmm, that's a fine distinction but defensible: the hypothesis is phrased as a claim about how GK MD behaves (a scientific fact claim), if_lever/if_not are phrased as the plan's expected measurements.\n\nHmm, honestly, both if_lever and if_not are also \"predictions about what the plan's experiment will show\" — same as hypothesis. If I exempt predictions, hypothesis should also be exempt... \n\nLet me re-read the hypothesis once more: \"Green–Kubo MD with one fixed force field has a chemistry-dependent error floor near 25 percent that more molecules cannot remove [s_ogbomo_res].\" The citation indicates the producer grounds this in the prior results. The claim structure: \"GK MD has an error floor near 25% that more molecules cannot remove\" — this is a claim about the *method's* behavior, motivated by the paper. In a plan, the hypothesis is what you predict you'll find. But the specific \"25 percent\" is presented as an empirical fact from the source (\"error floor near 25 percent\" — grounded in the paper's error magnitudes).\n\nDecision: PARTIAL. The quote supports \"chemistry-dependent force-field errors\" for viscosity/thermal conductivity but states no 25% error floor (the paper reports viscosity errors from small up to 137% at 40 °C, with four of eight within 25%) and says nothing about more molecules being unable to remove the error.\n\nFor if_lever/if_not, I'll say SUPPORTED as plan predictions with notes. Hmm... but you know, there's an argument they should be UNSUPPORTED if one reads them as claims that the source reports these outcomes. The \"[s_ogbomo_res]\" citation... \n\nLet me look at the \"supports\" field of src:s_ogbomo_res: \"anchor tolerances, the predicted error floor and the if_not expectation\". So the producer claims s_ogbomo_res supports \"the predicted error floor and the if_not expectation\"! That means the producer themselves treats the if_not expectation and the error floor as derived from s_ogbomo_res. So the source is claimed to support if_not's expectation (\"error stays above 25 percent and rank correlation below 0.7\"). Does the quote support \"rank correlation below 0.7 at every set size\"? No — the quote says ranking largely preserved for viscosity (which would imply high rank correlation!) and misordered for thermal conductivity. So the source arguably CONTRADICTS the if_not viscosity expectation: \"the reference ranking of the fluids is nonetheless largely preserved\" for viscosity suggests rank correlation is good, not below 0.7. Hmm! That's for viscosity ranking among the eight.\n\nInteresting: card:if_not says \"Held-out viscosity error stays above 25 percent and rank correlation below 