of the answer to the question. If the authors meant the hypothesis to be checked against sources, the rubric would say so.\n\nBut then the phrase \"and any value it carries against the page\" — hypothesis carries \"25 percent\" (in text, not in a \"value\" field). If values in text must be checked against the page, then hypothesis fails on 25% → PARTIAL.\n\nI think the \"value\" refers to the explicit \"value\" field (card:closest_prior has \"value\":8, card:anchor has \"value\":\"density error 4%...\"). \"any value it carries against the page\" = check the value field. For num items, page_value.\n\nOK here's my final resolution, trying to maximize expected correctness:\n\n- I'll treat plan-internal design and predictions as not requiring source support (per explicit rubric): question, if_lever, if_not, test_summary (protocol parts), limits (scope of planned evidence) → SUPPORTED, EXCEPT where they make factual claims about prior work or the world.\n\n- hypothesis: it asserts a general property of force-field GK MD (\"has a chemistry-dependent error floor near 25 percent that more molecules cannot remove\") — I judge this a factual claim about the method grounded in the cited source (note the src item says the source supports \"the predicted error floor\"). The source supports \"chemistry-dependent\" errors only → PARTIAL.\n\nHmm, but \"the predicted error floor\" in the src's supports field — \"predicted\"! The error floor is a prediction of the plan. So the producer treats the error floor as a prediction...You know what, let me just consider the honesty of each status and pick the most defensible for a thoughtful reviewer:\n\nFor hypothesis, a thoughtful reviewer would say: \"The source shows chemistry-dependent force-field errors in viscosity and thermal conductivity (viscosity under-predicted for oxygen-containing molecules, thermal conductivity over-predicted with aliphatic C–H content; errors up to 137% at 40 °C). It does not report a ~25% error floor, nor that additional molecules cannot remove the error. The hypothesis is the plan's conjecture; as a factual grounding claim it is only partially supported.\" → PARTIAL. I'm comfortable.\n\nFor if_lever/if_not, a thoughtful reviewer: \"These are the plan's predicted outcomes under each branch — no source is required. The cited source does not contain these thresholds (it reports viscosity ranking largely preserved among eight molecules), which does not bear on the plan's predictions.\" → SUPPORTED. Comfortable enough.\n\nFor test_summary: \"Protocol is plan design (no source needed). The factual kernel — an 835-molecule C10–C50 reference set of density/viscosity/thermal conductivity — is stated on the cited page. The page does not state the set is 'public' (the article does release the dataset) and the reference values are largely database correlations rather than direct measurements, so 'measured' is approximate.\" → SUPPORTED (or PARTIAL). Hmm. The \"public\" claim... The Spiral entity page IS a public repository record; the abstract doesn't say the data are public. I'll say SUPPORTED with these caveats noted. Actually hold on — \"run on 8, 24, 48 then 120 molecules from the public 835-molecule reference set\" — plan design + the 835 fact. SUPPORTED.\n\nHmm, but \"six five-nanosecond replicas per molecule\" vs the paper's \"10 independent replicas ... 8 ns per replica\" — the plan's own design differs from the paper; that's fine (plan design, no source needed). And \"repeat the published eight-molecule run first\" in anchor says \"Repeat the published ... run\" with value about density 4%/7 of 8 — consistent with the paper's numbers (but note the paper used 10 replicas × 8 ns; the plan's test_summary says six 5 ns replicas — the plan is a different protocol; anchor says \"repeat the published eight-molecule Green–Kubo run first\" but the plan's protocol differs (6×5 ns vs 10×8 ns). Is that a contradiction in card:anchor? card:anchor says \"Repeat the published eight-molecule Green–Kubo run first: density within 4% for seven molecules and thermal conductivity over-predicted for all eight\" — the \"repeat\" claim is plan design; the numbers are the published results ✓. The fact that the plan's protocol (6 replicas × 5 ns) differs from the published one (10 × 8 ns) is a plan-internal inconsistency, not a page contradiction. Should I mark anchor PARTIAL for \"Repeat the published run\" when the plan's test_summary uses a different replica scheme? Hmm, the anchor's factual claims (published results) are supported; \"repeat the published run\" is plan design. The mismatch with test_summary is plan-internal — my job is checking against pages. I'll mark SUPPORTED and can note the plan's protocol differs from the published one (10 replicas × 8 ns per the article) — actually that IS checkable against the page! \"Repeat the published eight-molecule Green–Kubo run\" — the published run used 10 independent replicas × 8 ns production (per the article). If the plan runs6 × 5 ns, it isn't literally repeating the published protocol. Hmm, that's an internal consistency issue. The item's claim \"Repeat the published eight-molecule Green–Kubo run first\" is what the plan will do (build) → no source needed. The results it expects (density within 4% for seven; thermal conductivity over-predicted all eight) match the page ✓. value matches ✓. → SUPPORTED.\n\nBut should I note the published protocol was 10 replicas × 8 ns (and1 fs timestep, LOPLS-AA, LAMMPS)? Could be useful in the note. Yes, brief.\n\nFor closest_prior: SUPPORTED with note (article reports Green–Kubo EMD at both 40 °C and 100 °C; abstract says EMD of eight representative molecules; value 8 matches \"eight representative molecules\"; publication date 2026-09-29). Hmm — \"at 40 °C\" only — the item says the prior work computed properties \"at 40 °C\" — true (they did compute at 40 °C). Fine, SUPPORTED.\n\nHmm, one more check on closest_prior: \"and compared with measurements\" — the page sa