endent under/over-prediction; ranking preserved/misordered) arguably touch on it but don't resolve it.\n\nI'll go PARTIAL: the cited page supports that a reference set of835 molecules exists (the \"hundreds of molecules\" scale) and the work reports chemistry-dependent errors for eight molecules, but it does not state that the bias-vs-floor question or rank agreement at that scale is left open; that framing is the plan's gap claim.\n\nHmm, or UNSUPPORTED. The core of left_open is \"left open\" = gap claim. The quote supports none of \"left open\". PARTIAL implies part supported — the \"hundreds of molecules\" scale is supported as a fact (835 molecules). I'll go PARTIAL.\n\nHmm, alternatively treat left_open as the plan's research-gap framing = what the plan will measure → no source → SUPPORTED. \n\nDecision rule I'll apply consistently: Does the item assert something about prior work/the world as fact? left_open asserts \"this is left open [by prior work]\" — yes, that's a literature claim. → needs support → PARTIAL/UNSUPPORTED. I'll pick UNSUPPORTED? Let me think: the claim \"whether the error is a correctable bias or a random floor that caps ranking\" is left open — the source (the Ogbomo paper) is the prior work; its abstract says nothing about leaving it open; the paper in fact reports that ranking is largely preserved (for viscosity) which partially addresses ranking behaviour. So the gap claim is not established by the cited quote. But it's not contradicted either (the paper does only8 molecules).\n\nPARTIAL: partially — the source establishes the 835-molecule reference scale but not that these questions remain open. I'll go PARTIAL. Fine.\n\nNow let me also double check card:anchor's value vs page: value = \"density error 4% or less on 7 of 8 molecules at 40 °C\" — page: \"Density is reproduced to within 4% for seven of the eight molecules at 40 °C.\" ✓ exactly. Also \"thermal conductivity over-predicted for all eight\" — page: \"Thermal conductivity is over-predicted for every molecule\" (of the eight) ✓. SUPPORTED.\n\nAnd the value check for card:closest_prior: value 8 — page: \"eight representative molecules\" ✓.\n\nNow num items status/page_value:\n\nnum:n_ref_molecules: 835 molecules — page states \"835 unique organic molecules in the C10–C50 range\" for the reference dataset ✓ → SUPPORTED, page_value 835.\n\nnum:n_gpus: 8 GPUs — page: Instances pricing tabs \"8x4x2x1x\"; the 8x tab table lists the A100 SXM 40 GB config. The quote \"Instances pricing (8x): ...\" is a condensed form. The page states the 8-GPU instance (8x tab) with that row. → SUPPORTED, page_value 8.\n\nHmm — is \"8 GPUs on the rented instance\" a cost input? \"For a cost input, check that the page states the number for the setting given\" — the setting: \"Lambda on-demand 8-GPU A100 SXM 40 GB instance\". The page's 8x tab lists \"NVIDIA A100 SXM | 40 GB | 124 | 1800 GiB | 5.8 TiB SSD | $1.99\" — under \"Instances pricing\" with GPU-count tabs 8x/4x/2x/1x. So 8 GPUs is stated (as the 8x tab). ✓ SUPPORTED, page_value 8.\n\nnum:a100x8_usd_gpu_h: 1.99 USD/GPU-hour for 8x A100 SXM 40 GB — page: \"$1.99\" under 8x tab for NVIDIA A100 SXM 40 GB ✓ → SUPPORTED, page_value 1.99.\n\nnum:throughput_ns_day: 250 ns/day assumed (PROXY). Page (Amber) states 611.08 for1×A100 JAC_PRODUCTION_NVE 23,558 atoms PME 2 fs ✓ quote appears. The page does not state 250; it states 611.08 for the given setting. → PARTIAL or CONTRADICTED with page_value 611.08.\n\nPer instruction \"When the page states a different number for a num or rate item, give it as page_value\" — I'll give 611.08. Status: The item's value (250) is not the page's number for the stated setting; the item discloses it's a derived proxy (safety factor 2.4). Is that \"PARTIAL\" or \"CONTRADICTED\"? The instruction's phrase \"when the page states a different number... give it as page_value\" suggests the status is then CONTRADICTED (the page states a different number). Hmm. But CONTRADICTED seems harsh for an